xnu: park an offline core where it cannot be powered down; idle accounting; deep idle

Taking a core offline (processor_exit, the sleep path) ends in cpu_sleep(), which on Apple
SoCs sets CYC_OVRD "ok to power down" with WFI retention disabled and FIQ/IRQ masked in the
same register, and executes WFI. The core loses power; cpu_exit_wait() polls its run state
in cpu-impl-reg until it reads 0; a later processor_start releases it from reset through the
IOPMGR. Under the m1n1 hypervisor none of that is available: hv_exc.c refuses a CYC_OVRD
write with DISABLE_WFI_RET or a FIQ mode, the core therefore keeps its power and its run
state, and the PMGR start hook can only start a core that is not yet in the guest
(hv_start_secondary sends work to m1n1's own loop on that core). Linux on this SoC does not
offline cores at all (enable-method spin-table has no cpu_die).

So an offline core is parked (sfcpupark, default on; sfcpupark=0 keeps xnu's path): after
everything else cpu_sleep() does, it switches its timer off and waits in WFI with interrupts
masked until PE_cpu_start_internal(), instead of calling IOPMGR::enableCPUCore, sets its
restart flag and sends it an IPI. It then switches to its start-up stacks and enters
arm_init_cpu() as start_cpu would after a reset. cpu_exit_wait() does not poll the run state
of a parked core. XNU_ENABLE_PROCESSOR_EXIT is set for T8103, so processor_exit() and
cpuctl work without the processor_exit=1 boot-arg.

Accounting, for the question "is an idle core waiting or spinning": machdep.sfcpu.stats has
one line per core with the number of idle WFIs, the time spent in them, how many returned
within 20 us, the CYC_OVRD value the core idles with, and the park counters (WFI returns
while parked).

Deep idle (sfidledeep=1 or machdep.sfcpu.deep_wfi=1, off by default): an idle WFI that the
next timer leaves at least 10 ms long is entered with CYC_OVRD WFI mode 3, the "CPU PD"
state of Linux's cpuidle-apple on this SoC (same register write, 10 ms target residency);
the register is put back on leaving idle. The hypervisor passes that write through.
UPSTREAM: the accounting and deep idle possibly; parking is for running without Apple's
power manager.

